Wait — what's the carried contextWindow for the model in roles mode? The chain ref has no contextWindow → catalog default for copilot = 1M (per the existing test: kimi carried 1M). So the model carries 1M and the endpoint confirms 1M.

But careful: what does `cli.agent.state.model` resolve to at waitForIt time — the boot model before/after any rebuild. The registry publish keys on identity; isVerified(model) with the resolved model — identity stays constant (copilot/gpt-4.1/baseUrl). The baseUrl of the roles-built model — copilot default endpoint. Both publish (with the fix) and isVerified use the same → fine.

One concern — `rolesCli` in that test file doesn't pass `env`/`sessionRoot`? It does (env: env, sessionRoot: '/sessions'). And it doesn't wait for REPL-up before run() — the existing tests `await waitForIt(...)` on outputs. My waitForIt on the registry works even while booting.

Also does `_applyDetectedContextWindow` get invoked in roles mode? Yes — the existing first test ("learns the real window") proves the path runs in roles mode.

Now the maxTokens: carried maxTokens for the catalog model — whatever; reporting 16384 may or may not differ; if it differs, _applyDetectedMaxTokens rebuilds (harmless).

Timing hazard: `_refreshModelCache` runs at boot; will it definitely complete before the 25s waitForIt? Yes (in-memory mock).

RED today: isVerified false → waitForIt times out (25s) → test fails with 'timed out waiting'. That's a slow RED (25s) but acceptable; actually — I can make the RED faster by inverting: run, wait a fixed short delay (like the pinned-roles test: `await Future<void>.delayed(const Duration(milliseconds: 200))`), then expect isVerified true. The pinned test uses that pattern for a NEGATIVE wait. For a positive assertion, polling is better (avoids flaky-under-load). I'll use waitForIt — the RED run costs 25s once.

