The local meter: `transcriptTokens = estimateContextTokens(messages).tokens` + `overheadOf = estimateRequestOverheadTokens(systemPrompt, tools)`. The overhead is ~8.5k for the real CLI (system prompt + tool schemas).

Now the arithmetic with the corrected curve:
- `_threshold` = 32768 − 8192 = 24576
- Default budget: messages priced by estimateContextTokens: 400 chars ≈ 100 tokens each (400/4 = 100! not 100 — I misread: textChars = 400, so ~100 tokens per message).

18 seeded + 'go' ≈ 19,000 transcript + overhead. What overhead does the headless CLI actually carry? ~8.5k → 19000 + 850 = 27500 > 24576 → trimmed to 15 kept ✓ matches the actual!

Pre-c1: threshold = 28672; 27500 ≤ 28672 → kept 19 ✓ matches the old expectation.

So with the corrected curve, 18 flat messages (~100 tok each) no longer fit the default budget. The intended test needs a file that fits the default budget and busts the override (16384 budget = window − 16384):

needed: estimate ≤ 24576 (default keeps) AND > 16384 (override trims). With ~100 tok/msg + 8.5k overhead: count messages n: 100n + 850 + 'go' ≤ 24576 → n ≤ 15.7 → n ≤ 15; and 100n + 850 > 16384 → n > 7.9 → n ≥ 8. So n ∈ [8..15] — e.g. **12** messages sits mid-range with margin on both sides (12×100+850 ≈ 20500 ≤ 24576 ✓; 20500 > 16384 ✓).

But hard-coding 12 re-introduces the same fragility (a future curve change stales it again — though any number must). Better: derive BOTH seeds from the local meter with the file's own idiom:

```dart
// The DEFAULT budget keeps the whole file, the LARGER pinned reserve
// (16384) trims it: pick the seed count with the file's own meter so
// both properties hold against the ACTIVE curve (this header's rule —
// gh-1742 review c1 corrected the forWindow curve and staled the old
// fixed 18).
int seedCountFor(int textChars) {
 Context probe(int count) => Context(messages: [
   for (var i = 0; i < count; i++) UserMessage.text('m$i ${'a' * textChars}'),
   const UserMessage.text('go'),
 ]);
 // The largest count that still fits the DEFAULT parity budget...
 var best = 0;
 for (var count = 1; count <= 40; count++) {
   if (requestTokens(probe(count)) > _threshold) break;
   best = count;
 }
 return best;
}
``
Then require the override bust: `requestTokens(probe(best)) > _window - 16384` — if the largest fitting file doesn't bust the override budget, the test's premise is unsatisfiable at this curve; make the failure explicit with a guard expect with a reason. With 400-char messages: best = 15 (15000+850=23500 ≤ 24576; 16 → 24500+850=33000 > 24576 → wait 16000+850=24500 ≤ 24576 → best=16; 17 → 25500+850=34000 > → stop). best=16: 24500 > 16384 ✓ busts override. 

Also the AC-R3 assertions update: `keptByDefault == seeded + 1` (whole file incl. 'go') — keep the exact `19`→ now `best+1`. And `keptByOverride < keptByDefault` stays.

Note: `requestTokens` in the test file takes a Context — the probe context has no systemPrompt/tools → overheadOf(probe) = estimateRequestOverheadTokens(null, []) ≈ 0!! The real CLI carries ~8.5k. So my probe must include the overhead — pass the CLI's real overhead: the IT-NOGUARD probe MEASURES it off a captured request. I can measure similarly: run ONE tiny headless resume (4 seeded messages), measure requestTokens(firstContext) − transcriptTokens(firstContext) = real overhead O. Then use O in the derivation. The E4 test pattern does single runs; each run takes a few seconds. Acceptable — and it makes the derivation honest.

